<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="camalexin biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="CAMALEXIN-SYN" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Arabidopsis thaliana col. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME: THIAZOL-YL-INDOLE Camalexin| is the main phytoalexin in |FRAME: TAX-3702|. It is involved in plant defense and has cancer-preventive properties. The accumulation of camalexin is induced by an attack by a variety of microorganisms including bacteria, fungi and oomycetes. Camalexin was previously proposed to be derived from indole-3-carboxaldehyde, independent of tryptophan. However, genetic evidence showed that camalexin is derived from tryptophan via |FRAME: INDOLE-3-ACETALDOXIME|, a branch point metabolite that also leads to the biosynthesis of the secondary metabolites glucosinolates (|FRAME: PWY-601|). Double-mutants of |FRAME: AT4G39950| and |FRAME: AT2G22330| were devoid of camalexin |CITS: [15148388]|. Indole-3-acetaldoxime is consequently converted to indole-3-acetonitrile (IAN) by |FRAME: AT2G30770| and CYP71A12 |CITS: [17573535][25953104]|. Early &lt;i&gt;in vivo&lt;/i&gt; feeding experiments suggested that the thiazole ring of camalexin is derived from cysteine |CITS: [9046593]|. However, it was later shown that the source of sulfur is glutathione |CITS: [21712415]|. Following the formation of the conjugate by |FRAME: AT1G02930| |CITS: [21239642]|, the &amp;gamma;-glutamyl residue of glutathione is removed by the cytosolic peptidases |FRAME: AT4G30530| and |FRAME: GQT-2803|. The glycine residue is assumed to be removed by an as-yet uncharacterized carboxypeptidase |CITS: [21712415]|. The last steps of camalexin biosynthesis are catalyzed by |FRAME: AT3G26830|, a multifunctional enzyme that forms |FRAME: INDOLE-CARBOXY-HYDRO-THIAZOLE| (DHCA) |CITS: [19567706]| and converts it to camalexin. CYP71B15 is the only known cytochrome P450 in plants that catalyzes both decarboxylation and introduction of a C-C double bond |CITS: [16766671]|. During the conversion of one molecule |FRAME: CPD-12636| to DHCA, one molecule of cyanide is released. In planta accumulation of cyanide may shut down the CYP P450 catalyzed camalexin biosynthesis process and thus cyanide is possibly detoxified via the cyanide detoxification pathway (|FRAME: ASPSYNII-PWY|). The biosynthetic pathway is induced coordinately, and induction is trictly localized to the site of pathogen infection |CITS: [17073786]|.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="zhang" />
  </Authors>
