    <DataNode elementId="GLUTATHIONE" textLabel="glutathione" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="RWSXRVCMGQZWBV-WDSKDSINSA-M" dataSource="InChIKey" />
      <Graphics centerX="350" centerY="620" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment>Thiols play a major role in the detoxification of stress-inducing factors such as reactive oxygen species, free radicals, peroxides, lipid peroxides, and heavy metals. In mammals, the major thiol is the tripeptide |FRAME: GLUTATHIONE| (&amp;gamma;-Glu-Cys-Gly, known as GSH). Toxins are conjugated to GSH in the liver by a dedicated enzyme, resulting in a |FRAME: S-Substituted-L-Cysteines &quot;cysteine-toxin conjugate&quot;|. The conjugate is acetylated in the kidney to form |FRAME: Mercapturates|, and is then excreted |CITS: [8145281]| (see |FRAME: PWY-4061|). In addition to oxidative stress management, low molecular weight thiols have been found to be involved in critical cellular processes including DNA synthesis and formaldehyde reduction |CITS: [Patel98]|. Gluathione is the major thiol not only in eukaryotic organisms but also in Gram negative bacteria and in the majority of Gram positive bacteria. Pathogenic bacteria live in a particularly hostile environment, in which the host deliberately generates toxins, including reactive oxygen species, to destroy the invading organism, thus thiols are particularly important for the survival of these organisms. Despite the ubiquity of GSH, it is not universal. In many organisms, including some eukaryotes, other thiols have taken its role. Some of these thiols are |FRAME: CoM|, |FRAME: TRYPANOTHIONE|, |FRAME: Ergothioneine|, |FRAME: CPD1G-2| and |FRAME: CPD-17028|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="GLUTATHIONE" />
